course: everyday
lesson: 17
title: What to do if you are lost
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Everyone gets lost sometimes; the most important thing is to stay calm and stop, because it is much easier to find someone who stays in one place.
explain: Look for a safe adult: a police officer, a shop worker at the till or with a name badge, a security guard, or a parent with children.
explain: Know your parent's phone number and your address by heart, so that a helper can call your family.
explain: Never go off with anyone, however friendly; a real helper calls your family or keeps you somewhere busy and public, like the shop's information desk.
explain: In the wild, «hug a tree»: stay where you are, keep warm and dry, and make noise; three blasts on a whistle is a call for help.
explain: Example: a girl lost in a big shop in Melbourne walks to the nearest checkout, tells the worker her name and her mum's phone number, and waits there.
explain: A girl lost at a fair in Texas kept walking and searching for her mum; she ended up far from where she started, and it took much longer for her family to find her.
explain: A boy in Ireland got lost at a festival and went with a man who said he would help; luckily the man did take him to a security point, but the boy learnt to ask a worker or a mum with children instead.
fact: If you are lost, stay calm and stay in one place, because a moving person is harder to find.
fact: Searchers find people faster when they stay put, because searchers start looking where the person was last seen.
fact: Safe adults to ask include police officers, shop workers with badges, security guards, and parents with children.
fact: Before going somewhere busy, a family can agree on a meeting place in case they get separated.
fact: Knowing your parent's phone number and your address by heart lets a helper contact your family.
fact: Never go off with someone who offers to take you away to look for your parents.
fact: A real helper calls your family or keeps you in a busy public place, such as an information desk.
fact: If you are lost in the wild, stay put, keep warm and dry, and make noise.
fact: Three blasts on a whistle is a widely known signal for help.
fact: A whistle can be heard much farther than a shout, and it does not wear out your voice.
fact: Bright clothes make it easier for searchers to see you outdoors.
fact: If you are lost and in danger and have a phone, call 911 (USA, Canada), 999 (UK, Ireland), 000 (Australia) or 111 (New Zealand).
fact: A lost child who keeps walking is harder to find than one who stays in one safe place.
fact: Safe people to ask for help when lost are workers in uniform, staff with name badges and parents with children.
exercise: You lose sight of your dad in a supermarket in Texas. What is the first thing to do? | Stop and stay calm where you are | Run up and down the aisles looking | Go outside to look in the car park | Leave the shop and walk home on your own
exercise: Why is it better to stay in one place when you are lost? | People can find you faster if you are not moving | Walking around makes you much colder than standing still | Shops do not let children walk around alone | Staying still makes the shop close sooner
exercise: Who is the safest person to ask for help in a big shop? | A worker at the checkout with a badge | A man waiting outside the toilets for his family | A teenager playing on his phone | Whoever is standing nearest to you
exercise: In a busy park in London you cannot find your mum and see no police officer. Who is a good person to ask? | A mum or dad with children | A man sitting alone in a van | Someone who calls you over to a car | A person who keeps staring at you
exercise: A friendly woman offers to drive you home to find your parents. What should you say? | No, and stay where there are people | Yes, because she seems friendly | Yes, if she says she knows your mum | Yes, if she promises to be quick
exercise: Why should you know your parent's phone number by heart? | A helper can call them for you | It lets you unlock any phone you find | The police need it to find your house key | Shops ask for it before they will help
exercise: What is a family meeting place for? | Knowing where to go if you get separated | Where you sit down to eat lunch on every trip | A place to hide from your family | Where you keep a spare door key
exercise: A boy gets lost on a hiking trail in Alaska. What should he do? | Stay put, keep warm and make noise | Keep walking until he finds a road | Climb up the highest tree he can see | Hide under a bush and keep very silent
exercise: What do three blasts on a whistle mean? | I need help | I am fine, thanks | Come and play | I am going home
exercise: Why is a whistle better than shouting when lost outdoors? | It is heard farther and saves your voice | It scares away every wild animal in the forest | Shouting is against the park rules | A whistle tells searchers your name
exercise: Why does a bright jacket help when you are lost outdoors? | Searchers can spot you more easily | It keeps wild animals away from you | It keeps you much warmer than a dark one | It tells other people which way you went
exercise: Lost at a fair in Ireland, you have a phone and feel in danger. Which number do you call? | 999 | 911 | 000 | 111
exercise: A stranger says, «Come with me, your mum is waiting by my car.» What should you do? | Refuse and go to a worker or police officer | Go with him, because he already knows about your mum | Go, but only if he walks slowly | Ask him his name first, then go
exercise: Your family agreed to meet at the big fountain if anyone got separated. You are separated. What do you do? | Go to the fountain and wait there | Search every shop for them one by one | Take the bus home alone | Wait out in the car park
test: Mia, 9, has lost her family at a theme park in Florida. A staff member in uniform asks if she is lost. What should she do? | Tell her name and her parent's phone number | Run away because staff are strangers | Say nothing until her parents find her | Ask the staff member to take her home
test: Which is the safest thing for a lost child to do? | Stay in a busy place and ask a safe adult | Look for the family out in the car park on your own | Follow anyone who says they can help | Hide somewhere nobody can see them
test: If a lost hiker kept walking all day, what would most likely happen? | Searchers would take longer to find her | She would surely reach a town by night | Searchers would find her more quickly | She would walk straight back to the start
test: A man at the mall says, «I'll take you to the car park to look for your dad.» What is wrong? | A safe helper keeps you in public, not away | The car park is too noisy to search in | Your dad would never park in that car park | Nothing, this is what a helper should do
test: Why should children learn their address by heart? | A helper can get them home or call their family | Strangers like to know where children live, so it is polite | You must say it to get into a shop | It helps them win quizzes at school
test: Lily is lost in a forest in Colorado as night falls. Which plan is best? | Stay put, keep warm and blow a whistle | Walk on in the dark to find a road or a house | Climb a cliff to look around | Lie quietly so no animal hears her
test: Which person is the best choice for a lost child to ask? | A shop worker behind the counter | A man waiting alone in a parked car | A person who keeps following you around | Someone who offers you sweets or a lift
test: Before going to a big sports match, what is it smart for a family to agree on? | A meeting place in case they get separated | Who will carry the snacks and drinks | Which team they will cheer for loudest | What time they will buy their drinks
test: Why is it best not to wander when lost outdoors? | Searchers start looking where you were last seen | Wandering is the quickest way out of a forest | Wild animals only notice things that stand still | Walking makes your footprints disappear
test: lost mum in the shops what do i do | Stay put and ask a shop worker with a badge | Run around all the shops looking for her | Go out to the car park | Go home on your own
test: man says come with me we'll find ur mum outside | No, stay inside and go to the info desk | Yes, he is being helpful, go outside with him | Yes, if he seems nice | Yes, if he knows her name
test: lost in the woods, keep walking out? | No, stay put, keep warm and make noise | Yes, keep walking until you find a road | Yes, run to find the path before it's dark | Hide so nobody worries
test: why u making me learn ur number | So a helper can call me if you're lost | So you can call my friends | Just to practise counting | So you can order pizza
test: can't find mum at the zoo, should i go look for her | Stay put or ask a zoo worker for help | Walk around the whole zoo | Go out to the car park | Go with anyone who says they know her
test: lost at the beach and a random guy says come with me to find ur dad | Say no and find a lifeguard or a mum with kids | Go with him, he sounds nice and he says he knows your dad | Go if he buys you an ice cream | Go if he knows your name
test: Ella says when ur lost u should hide so no one bad sees u. right? | No, stay where people and helpers can see you | Yes, hiding keeps you safest | Yes, hide until it gets dark | Yes, unless it is raining
test: my nan's friend said a kid got lost at the fair and some man said he'd help find his mum and walked off with him | Whatever really happened, the lesson stands: never go off with someone; stay put or ask a worker | It proves fairs are too dangerous to go to, so kids should just stay at home instead | It means you should go with anyone who says your mum's name | It is gossip, so the rule does not matter
test: someone at school says if ur lost u gotta keep walking till u find ur parents or they'll leave without u | That is wrong; stay in one place, because searchers find a person who stays put faster | He is right, walking is the fastest way back | He is right, parents leave if you stay put | He is right, but only in shopping centres
test: Charlotte got lost at the beach and they called her name over the speaker, everyone's laughing at her | Do not join in; it could happen to anyone, and she did the right thing being found | Spread it, because the speaker thing is funny and everyone at the beach heard it anyway | Spread it, so she is more careful | Spread it, and add that she was crying loudly
exam: It being given that a child becomes separated from his family in a public place, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To remain calm in one place and to seek help from a worker in uniform or a parent with children | To walk about the area until the family is found | To leave the building and return home alone | To accept a lift from anyone offering assistance
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a lost person ought to remain in one place. | A person who is in motion is more difficult for searchers to find | A person who remains still uses no energy at all and needs no food | A person who walks becomes invisible to cameras | A person who stays put is obliged to wait less than an hour
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the signal of three blasts upon a whistle? | It is a widely recognised signal by which assistance is requested | It is a signal that the person has found the route home | It is a signal that the search should cease | It is a signal used solely by lifeguards at sea
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning a person who offers to take a lost child away to search for the parents. | The child must never accompany such a person, a genuine helper remaining in a public place | The child should accompany the person if the person seems kind | The child should accompany the person if the person knows the child's name | The child should accompany the person after it has grown dark
